<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<TcPlcObject Version="1.1.0.1">
  <POU Name="F_SafeBPCompare0Rate" Id="{61333098-b080-43b8-8c0d-b267fc83ae7a}" SpecialFunc="None">
    <Declaration><![CDATA[(* F_SafeBPCompare0Rate
Compares two beam parameter sets and answers whether BeamParam1 is at least as conservative as
BeamParam2, i.e. whether the beam parameters currently in effect are safe under the assertion
BeamParam2 describes.

Usage - a FUNCTION, so it holds no state and may be called from any task:
    xTranBP := F_SafeBPCompare0Rate(stCurrentBeamParameters, i_stTransitionAssertion);
Assertion status is deliberately not considered here: the caller must also confirm that
BeamParam2 is active in the arbiter (e.g. FB_Arbiter.CheckRequest).

Objectives:
- Give the beam parameter transition manager one "is the beam safe right now" decision to make
  before it advances to the next transition state.
- Treat a machine that is not delivering beam as unconditionally safe, because the plain
  comparison would otherwise let beam back while the machine is not ready: in NC mode
  (stCurrentBeamParameters.nMachineMode = 0) a zero pulse rate, and in SC mode (= 1) a
  BeamParam1.nBCRange of 1 or less, mask every other consideration.
- Keep every other rule - transmission, photon energy, pulse rate, beam class, attenuators and
  apertures - in exactly one place by delegating to F_SafeBPCompare.
- Ignore status and veto devices; those are evaluated elsewhere.

Operand meaning and units, for both inputs (members of ST_BeamParams):
    nTran                          transmission, fraction 0..1 (0 = fully attenuated)
    nRate                          pulse rate, Hz (0 .. 1E6)
    neV, neVRange                  photon energy, eV, and the bitmask of allowed energy ranges
    nBCRange                       bitmask of allowed beam classes
    astAttenuators[n].nTran        attenuator transmission, %
    astApertures[n].Width/Height   slit separation, mm

History:
- A. Wallace 2020-09-10: Created. Split out of F_SafeBPCompare to avoid the pitfall of allowing
  beam back when the machine is not ready.
- ghalym 2023-01-31: Added the machine-mode gate to the zero-rate (NC) and zero-beam-class (SC)
  shortcuts.
- 2026-11-27: Documentation only - added the objectives and history sections and documented both
  operands (meaning and units). No behavioural change.
*)
FUNCTION F_SafeBPCompare0Rate : BOOL
VAR_INPUT
    (* Candidate set under evaluation; normally the beam parameters currently in effect. Uses the
    members and units listed in the header (nTran fraction 0..1, nRate Hz, neV/neVRange eV,
    nBCRange beam-class bitmask, attenuator nTran in %, aperture Width/Height in mm). This is the
    set that must be no more permissive than BeamParam2 for the comparison to return TRUE. *)
    BeamParam1    :    ST_BeamParams;
    (* Reference set that constrains BeamParam1; normally the transition or final assertion the
    machine has to satisfy. Same members and units as BeamParam1, compared member-wise by
    F_SafeBPCompare. Whether it is actually asserted in the arbiter is checked by the caller. *)
    BeamParam2    :    ST_BeamParams;
END_VAR
VAR

    // Beam-rate is zero, masks all others considerations.
    xZeroRate: BOOL;
    //Beam class is zero i.e beam off, masks all other considerations.
    xZeroBC : BOOL;

END_VAR
]]></Declaration>
    <Implementation>
      <ST><![CDATA[
xZeroRate  := (BeamParam1.nRate = 0) AND (PMPS_GVL.stCurrentBeamParameters.nMachineMode = 0);
xZeroBC  := (BeamParam1.nBCRange <= 1) AND (PMPS_GVL.stCurrentBeamParameters.nMachineMode =1);

F_SafeBPCompare0Rate := (xZeroRate OR xZeroBC) OR F_SafeBPCompare(BeamParam1, BeamParam2);
]]></ST>
    </Implementation>
  </POU>
</TcPlcObject>